course: dictionary
lesson: 6611
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. Norman can be a noun, an adjective or a name. In «In 1066 the Normans from France conquered England», Normans is a noun meaning the people from Normandy. The capital letter and the sentence about conquest are your clues.
explain: Look at the word that comes next. In «Norman castles», Norman is an adjective. It tells you what kind of castles they are: castles in the style of the Normans. Adjectives describe nouns, so if Norman comes in front of a thing, it is probably describing that thing.
explain: Norman can also be a man's first name, such as Norman Smith, or a family name. A name refers to a person, not a people or a style of building. Ask yourself: is it describing something, naming a group of people, or naming a person? Then put your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: Norman can be a noun, an adjective or a name, so always check the context.
fact: «The Normans» means the people from Normandy in northern France, who were led by William the Conqueror.
fact: «Norman castles» means castles built in the Norman style.
fact: Norman French added many words to English, such as beef, pork, judge and castle.
fact: Norman is a man's first name and also a surname, so it starts with a capital letter.
exercise: «In 1066 the Normans from France conquered England.» What does Normans mean here? | Castles with tall towers | A kind of French cheese | *The people from Normandy in France | Farmers who kept cows and sheep
exercise: What does Norman mean in «Norman castles»? | Made of wood and straw | *In the style of the Normans | A man called Norman | Very old and falling down
exercise: Which word came into English from Norman French? | *beef | sheep | cow | pig
test: «Norman is a man's first name.» What is Norman in this sentence? | *A name | A style of building | A people
test: What does Norman mean in «Norman French»? | *The French the Normans spoke | A man who lives in France | A tall, grey castle | A French cheese
exam: In «the Norman lords ate the meat», what kind of word is Norman? | A verb, showing an action | An adverb, describing how they ate | A noun, naming a country | *An adjective, describing the lords
